@prefix proeth-core: <http://proethica.org/ontology/core#> .
@prefix bfo: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_> .
@prefix iao: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_> .
@prefix ro: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_> .
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix time: <http://www.w3.org/2006/time#> .

# Ontology Declaration
<http://proethica.org/ontology/core> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "ProEthica Core Ontology"@en ;
    dc:creator "ProEthica AI"@en ;
    dc:date "2026-02-19"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment "Core ontology defining the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs) with CodeProvision extension for ethics code integration."@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "2.10.23"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.23: annotation editorial revision. Displayed annotations (rdfs:comment, iao:0000115, iao:0000116, skos:scopeNote) restated as descriptions of the current vocabulary, and the change-note history restated as vocabulary evolution. No logical axioms changed."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.22: documentation revisions on the Ca and Cs components. usesResource, constrainedBy, and realizesCapability are documented as reserved rather than deprecated. The Dennis et al. (2016) specification claim is restated in the terms of the paper (specification into precise context-dependent constraints, not complete specification). The Capability editor note states the competence-kind mechanism (the kind rides the class label through the match decision) and the CompetenceGapState route for lacked competences; hasCapability records that committed data assert the possessedBy direction. The capability kind count is corrected to nineteen."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.21: the Capability scope note names the incoming requiresCapability edge (the earlier requiredForObligation wording named a carrier field, not the edge). Annotation-only."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.20: citation layer on the Cs and Ca components. The Constraint definition sources are completed: the Dennis et al. entry carries the real title, venue, and identifier (its title slot formerly held a paraphrase), the Arkin and Ganascia records gain locators and identifiers, Benzmüller et al. (2020) is added for the formal-verification clause (re-homed from EthicalConstraint, whose differentia the paper does not support), and dcterms:source identifiers are added. The Arkin and Ulam author form is corrected to Arkin et al. (the 2012 paper is three-authored). The Capability editor note credits the four ethical-competence requirements to the analysis drawing on the Tolmeijer et al. survey (the labels do not occur in the survey itself), and the Tolmeijer and Epstein-Hundert records gain locators and identifiers."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.19: triggersEvent marked owl:deprecated (unused; proeth:causedByAction, the CausalChain family, and the Allen temporal machinery carry its directions). The isPerformedBy completeness restriction gains a corpus scope note: precedent-case actions resolve to no case Agent by design, a correct open-world none-select."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.18: Event and Action annotation accuracy. The Event editor note names event_type as the origin routing input and no longer lists triggersEvent among the committed case-individual links; both scope notes carry the proeth-core prefix for the fluent edges. Spelling normalized (fulfillment)."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.17: citation layer on the A and E components. The Sarmiento PRIMA record is corrected to 2022 with the full locator (the prior 2023 read the date of the arXiv extended version onto the conference paper); volitional is restated in the vocabulary of the source (an action follows from a volition) and the agentless connotation is credited through Sarmiento to Russell and Norvig. The Action editor note re-scopes its responsibility-attribution clause to what each source states: the imputability line is Sarmiento quoting Beebee, the NESS cause-in-fact test is Wright (1985), which Sarmiento formalizes, causality-fault-duty are the three bases of accountability of Kroll, and Floridi and Sanders separate accountability from intentionality-requiring responsibility. The Event emergency claim is re-anchored to Almpani, Stefaneas, and Frangos (emergency contingency protocols), a different work from the State-side Almpani and Stefaneas 2022. Both classes gain iao:0000119 definition-source records with dcterms:source identifiers."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.16: refersToDocument marked owl:deprecated (unused; containsProvision is the provision linkage and documentTitle the document-identity carrier). The citedByAgent comment names the Discussion-section authority signal; its dated range-broadening history moves to a change note. establishes keeps its IRI-only McLaren source (methodological derivation, not per-definition support)."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.15: Resource citation layer. The malformed dual definition source on Resource is split into per-source records: McLaren with the Artificial Intelligence 150 locator and DOI, Davis with the Philosophy & Public Affairs locator (pre-DOI era; JSTOR stable IRI as identifier), Frankel with the Journal of Business Ethics locator and DOI. The editor note no longer attributes the conventions-that-coordinate account jointly: convention and coordination are Davis's own words and absent from Frankel, whose contribution is the profession as a normative reference group. dcterms:source identifiers added."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.14: activatedByEvent and terminatedByEvent asserted as subproperties of the inverses of initiates and terminates, making the documented generalization reasoner-visible (the two families remain independently populated evidence channels); hasState marked owl:deprecated (unused; the v2.4.2 rationale preserved). The fluent family gains its literature tier (Kowalski and Sergot with the New Generation Computing locator; Berreby with the AAMAS record), with each vocabulary scoped to its owner (fluent is not a Kowalski and Sergot word; happening is the umbrella term of this design). activatesConstraint stated in the full relational form. The State editor note and scope note no longer claim an OWL-Time anchor on state individuals (state boundaries are the event edges plus the activePeriod literal). Style: caps emphasis lowercased, possessives moved to the of form."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.13: the State definition and scope note individuation lists restructured. Annotation-only."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.12: the three State definition-source strings completed with verified records (Jones with the Academy of Management Review locator and full page range; Berreby with the AAMAS proceedings pages; Anderson and Anderson in the settled Paladyn form); State gains dcterms:source identifiers. Annotation-only."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.11: the defeasibility property family gains its literature base, the lineage of the framework itself. The prima facie duty account grounds the structure (Ross and Stratton-Lake 2007: several duties bear on one situation, the more stringent becomes the duty proper in the circumstances) and Anderson and Anderson 2011 the computational formalization. competesWith and prevailsOver cite both; defeasibleUnder cites Ross for circumstance-dependence alongside the existing Kant-Stenseke clause. The structure-over-resolution design choice stays unattributed design language. Annotation-only."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.10: three pairwise owl:propertyDisjointWith axioms on fulfillsObligation, violatesObligation, and raisesObligation make the per-action engagement partition reasoner-visible. Source-fidelity corrections: the obligatedParty by-whom slot is credited to Richardson 1990 as adopted by Dennis et al. 2016; the requiresCapability and defeasibleUnder capacity clauses name the ought-implies-can principle of Kant with Stenseke 2024 as its computational analogue, and the defeasibility consequence is stated as a design extension rather than attributed. The participant-block Board fallback is scoped to invokedBy (with citedByAgent in the actor-edge family). The fulfillsObligation v2.3.0 note no longer narrows the RO participant range to independent continuants; the v2.7.2 same-reason parenthetical becomes complementary-reason. prevailsOver gains the dated change note for its 2026-05-23 characteristics. Style: activatesObligation stated in the full relational form, exemplar lists rebalanced, caps emphasis lowercased."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.9: Obligation annotation corrections. The editor-note Donohue parenthetical carries his verbatim differentia, issued with normative authority, in place of a contrast that occurs nowhere in the paper. The Dennis citation string gains pages and DOI and the Donohue string the CEUR venue form; Obligation gains dcterms:source identifiers alongside the citation strings. Style: type lowercased, individuation lists restructured, the v2.3.0 venue named precisely."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.8: Principle gains dcterms:source identifiers for its five definition sources (McLaren, Frankel, Morley, Taddeo, Prem), completing the convention that the iao:0000119 string is the human citation and the dcterms:source IRI the machine identifier. Annotation-only."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.7: citesAuthority deleted (unused; its Board-citation function is carried by citesProvision joined to establishes on the NSPE Code ontology and by the dcterms:source case citations on discovered classes). The derivedFromPrinciple design gloss is reattributed from Hallamaa and Kalliokoski 2022, whose article contains neither the construct nor its vocabulary, to Dennis et al. 2016, the citation the framework uses for principles specifying concrete obligations. The participant-edges block comment is rewritten to the current contract: extracted who fields resolve at commit into the object edges, no intermediate-namespace literal twin is committed, and a Board-pattern party resolves deterministically to the case-scoped NSPE Board Agent. The Constraint editor note counts ten boundary-type classes (PriorityConstraint deleted 2026-07-05). establishes gains the McLaren 2003 dcterms:source from the deleted intermediate twin pair. The deprecated intermediate counterparts of the five v2.8.0 promotions were deleted 2026-07-04 once no case referenced them. Style: spelling normalized, chain notation normalized to R->P->O."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.6: Principle source-fidelity corrections. The open-textured characterization is reattributed from Prem 2023 to McLaren 2003 (the term does not occur in Prem; the McLaren abstract pairs abstract with open-textured), with the Prem citation rescoped to the high-level, context-free statement of principles that Prem does support. The v2.3.0 note clause on Frankel is aligned to the source construct (aspirational codes state ideals; Frankel describes three code types along a continuum, not levels of a hierarchy), and the constitutional analogy says constitutional principles, the term of the source, naming its origin (Morley et al. 2021, co-cited with Taddeo et al. 2024). The rdfs:comment no longer places prescribe under the McLaren citation and refers to accumulated cases rather than case precedents (McLaren denies precedential force to Board of Ethical Review cases). The five iao:0000119 strings normalized to the venue, volume, issue, and pages format; Morley et al. 2021 added to the definition sources. No logical axioms changed."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.5: R-component property annotations revised; no logical axioms changed. The hasObligation comment is universalized (the former wording predated the v2.10.0 Role universalization and contradicted committed participant-side edges; the Role-wide domain enables the edge without committing existence). The actor-edge comments (hasClient, professionalPeerOf, employedBy) lead with meaning; the reviewsWorkOf and isRoleOf version history moves to property-level change notes. adheresToPrinciple gains the R-to-P-to-O applicability clause. The Role editor note states that four Kong identity-role categories are carried as the relational archetypes (a selection, not the full Kong inventory)."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.4: Role annotation corrections. The editor note no longer attributes the professional-versus-participant contrast to Oakley and Cocking 2001 (the word participant does not occur in their book; the contrast is a design construct of this ontology, introduced in v2.10.0) and names the Doernberg and Truog 2023 concept by their own term, spheres of morality. The scope-note Davis 1991 parenthetical gains the page cite (p. 157 n. 7, the footnote on unlicensed practice under the manufacturer's exemption). Style: spelling normalized, the rdfs:comment list reduced to duties or stakes, the per-occupant example list extended with experience, the three iao:0000119 strings given uniform venue and publisher detail. No logical axioms changed."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.3: the five typed actor-relation subproperties (hasClient, professionalPeerOf, employedBy, reviewsWorkOf, workReviewedBy) gain rdfs:domain and rdfs:range proeth-core:Role, replacing the schema:domainIncludes soft hints. The hints existed because earlier case data attached these edges to Agent endpoints, which the Agent/Role disjointness would have made inconsistent under hard domains; no such endpoints remain, so the axioms are sound. reviewsWorkOf gains the owl:inverseOf workReviewedBy axiom its comment had claimed. relatedTo deliberately stays domain-less as the unvetted-relationship fallback."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.2: representation-only consolidation of the pairwise disjointness block. The eleven Agent separations, previously spread over six mixed-direction owl:disjointWith statements, are one Agent-subject statement, and CodeProvision keeps its own statement against Resource. The unordered disjoint-pair set is identical before and after (12 pairs); no logical axioms changed. Agent is deliberately not folded into the nine-way owl:AllDisjointClasses, which expresses the component mutual-exclusion claim itself; see the comment at the disjointness block."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.1: style revision of the nine component-class editor notes: semicolon chains split into sentences, inanimate possessives moved to the of form, spelling normalized to American English. The extraction boundary and tie-break sentences are unchanged. No logical axioms changed."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.0: Role universalized to cover participant roles. The Role annotations state the universal genus (a role borne by an agent in virtue of a position or standing in professional practice) with obligation-generation attributed to ProfessionalRole; the core-level Role subClassOf (hasObligation some Obligation) restriction is removed (the identical restriction on proeth:ProfessionalRole in the intermediate layer carries the axiom), so participant roles (Client, Owner, affected public) no longer inherit an obligation-existence commitment their own definitions deny. One logical axiom removed; monotonic weakening."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.9.0: extraction boundary and tie-break sentences added to the nine component-class editor notes (Principle and Obligation prohibition contrast, Obligation firmness and compliance redirect, Constraint negative-form single-home rule, Event and AutomaticEvent origin tie-break, State stative-label and per-condition granularity, Action omission license, Principle per-value granularity). The Principle and Obligation provision-individuation claims are softened (provision anchoring is carried by the curated nspeReference annotations and the citesProvision edges, not by type individuation), and the State typing rationale moves from iao:0000115 into its change note. No logical axioms changed."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.8.0: Promoted five D-tuple-level edges from the intermediate namespace into core so each relation family is single-namespace: derivedFromPrinciple (Obligation to Principle), violatesObligation (Action to Obligation), guidedByPrinciple (Action to Principle), and raisesObligation (Action to Obligation), joining fulfillsObligation, realizesCapability, initiates, and terminates, which were already core. requiresCapability is promoted to core and re-domained from the former intermediate Role to Capability form to Obligation to Capability, encoding the deontic principle that an obligation presupposes the capacity to discharge it (Stenseke 2024); the intermediate Role-domain requiresCapability is deprecated. The superseded intermediate properties are marked owl:deprecated with rdfs:seeAlso pointing at the core equivalent, and the deprecated intermediate fulfillsObligation equivalentProperty bridge is deleted. The nine-way AllDisjointClasses backbone and the Event origin subclasses (AgentCausedEvent, ExogenousEvent, AutomaticEvent), both added earlier in the 2.x series, are unchanged."@en ;
    owl:imports <http://proethica.org/ontology/foundation> ;
    owl:imports <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/bfo.owl> ;
    owl:imports <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/iao.owl> ;
    owl:imports <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ro.owl> ;
    owl:imports <http://www.w3.org/2006/time#> ;
    dc:description "Version 2.7.2 grounds the role and action relations in the OBO Relations Ontology and imports the curated proethica-foundation stub (a BFO/IAO/RO subset declared locally so the alignment resolves and is reasoned). hasRole was already subPropertyOf ro:has_role; v2.7.2 adds isRoleOf (inverse of hasRole) subPropertyOf ro:role_of, and grounds performsAction subPropertyOf ro:participates_in and isPerformedBy subPropertyOf ro:has_participant. The normative participant edges (obligatedParty, constrainedEntity, invokedBy) are deliberately NOT grounded under ro:has_participant: their subject is an information content entity, not a process, so process-participation does not apply (the complementary reason v2.3.0 removed has_participant from fulfillsObligation: there the obligation object, a directive information entity, is not a process participant). Version 2.7.1 asserts Agent disjointWith Role, Capability, and Resource explicitly. These separations would follow from the BFO alignment (material entity versus role, disposition, and information content entity), but the validation harness strips owl:imports and does not load BFO, so without the explicit axiom an individual could be typed both Agent and Role (or Capability, or Resource) with no consistency violation. Agent was already disjoint with Action, Event, State, Principle, Obligation, and Constraint. Version 2.7.0 adds the canonical Event Calculus fluent-transition object properties initiates and terminates (a happening, Action or Event, initiates or terminates a State; the Initiates/Terminates machinery of Kowalski and Sergot 1986, carried over fluents by Berreby et al. 2017), generalizing the State-side activatedByEvent / terminatedByEvent to both happening kinds in the happening->State direction so the fluent layer connects the temporal components to the normative ones (Action/Event initiates State, State activatesObligation). Version 2.6.0 adds four participant object properties (obligatedParty, constrainedEntity, possessedBy, invokedBy) that wire the extracted 'who bears / is constrained by / possesses / invokes' fields of Obligation, Constraint, Capability, and Principle into first-class edges to the case Agent, joining the actor-edge family (availableTo, citedByAgent, affects). Their range is Agent, which is outside the nine disjoint D-tuple categories, so each edge is OWL-DL safe; possessedBy is declared owl:inverseOf hasCapability. Version 2.5.0 adds three defeasibility object properties (competesWith, prevailsOver, defeasibleUnder) that expose obligation competition as first-class structural relationships between typed individuals, supporting the architectural claim that defeasible patterns are queryable via SPARQL and available to external non-monotonic engines. Version 2.3.0 corrected BFO/IAO superclass alignments following formal audit against BFO 2020 (ISO/IEC 21838-2) and IAO definitions: Resource from independent continuant to information content entity; State from quality to specifically dependent continuant; Obligation refined to directive information entity (Donohue 2017); Principle and Constraint refined to directive information entity based on their prescriptive character (Frankel 1989, Ganascia 2007, Dennis et al. 2016); Capability from realizable entity to disposition."@en .

###############################################################
# Core Formal Specification Classes -- BFO 2020 / IAO Aligned (v2.3.0)
###############################################################

# R - Role: Professional roles with BFO alignment
proeth-core:Role a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000023 ;  # BFO:role (a realizable entity realized in processes of professional practice)
    rdfs:label "Role"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A position or standing borne by an agent in professional practice, with the duties or stakes that standing carries; the R component of D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A realizable role (BFO) borne by an agent in virtue of a position or standing in professional practice, which determines (filters) the principles and obligations that apply to the agent in that standing. A professional role additionally generates obligations teleologically from the distinctive good the profession serves; a participant role (a client or owner, or an affected member of the public) carries standing in the case without profession-generated duties. The role is individuated by the principles and obligations it determines and the audience relationships it establishes, not by the occupant's attributes. This is the R component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Operationalizes the teleological account of professional roles (Oakley and Cocking 2001), under which the obligations of a professional role emerge from the distinctive good of the profession. Within this ontology, that obligation-generation distinguishes a professional role from a participant role. Carries the identity virtues of Kong et al. (2020) and four of their identity-role categories as the relational archetypes, and admits the multiple spheres of morality Doernberg and Truog (2023) locate within a single profession."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Obligation-generation is axiomatized on ProfessionalRole (proeth:ProfessionalRole subClassOf (hasObligation some Obligation)), not on Role itself; a participant role carries no obligation-existence commitment. Relationship categories and virtues are definitional of the role type; per-occupant facts (license, specialty, employer, experience) attach to the role individual, not the class (cf. Davis 1991, p. 157 n. 7; most US engineers are unlicensed yet remain role-bound, so license is not role-defining)."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.0: universalized to cover participant roles; the professional-only genus contradicted the professional-versus-participant classification and the committed participant individuals. Obligation-generation moved to the ProfessionalRole axiom in the intermediate layer."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Oakley and Cocking (2001). Virtue Ethics and Professional Roles. Cambridge University Press." ,
                "Kong et al. (2020). Corpus-based empirical approach to professionalism: Identifying interactional roles and dispositions in professional codes of ethics. Journal of Applied Linguistics and Professional Practice 14(1): 3-28." ,
                "Doernberg and Truog (2023). Spheres of Morality: The Ethical Codes of the Medical Profession. The American Journal of Bioethics 23(12): 8-22." .

# P - Principle: Ethical principles as directive information entities
proeth-core:Principle a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000033 ;  # IAO:directive information entity
    rdfs:label "Principle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A directive information entity representing ethical values and guidelines for conduct, prescribing professional behavior; principles gain operational meaning extensionally through the accumulated cases that instantiate them (McLaren 2003); the P component of D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A directive information entity (IAO) that expresses an ethical value or standard of professional conduct, prescribing behavior with a world-to-word direction of fit. A principle is an abstract, open-textured value individuated by the value it expresses, not by any occasion of its invocation or by the code provision that states it; it gains concrete operational meaning extensionally, through the accumulated professional-code cases that instantiate it (McLaren 2003). This is the P component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The principle type is defined by the value it expresses alone, not by who invokes or balances it in a given case, nor by the code provision that states it; provision anchoring is carried by the curated nspeReference annotations and the commit-time citesProvision edges, and per-occasion facts (invocation, application target, balancing, resolution narrative) attach to the case individuals and edges, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "McLaren (2003). Extensionally defining principles and cases in ethics: An AI model. Artificial Intelligence 150(1-2): 145-181." ,
                "Frankel (1989). Professional Codes: Why, How, and with What Impact? Journal of Business Ethics 8(2-3): 109-115." ,
                "Taddeo et al. (2024). From AI Ethics Principles to Practices: A Teleological Methodology to Apply AI Ethics Principles in the Defence Domain. Philosophy & Technology 37(1): 42 (doi:10.1007/s13347-024-00710-6)." ,
                "Prem (2023). From ethical AI frameworks to tools: a review of approaches. AI and Ethics 3(3): 699-716 (doi:10.1007/s43681-023-00258-9)." ,
                "Morley et al. (2021). Ethics as a Service: A Pragmatic Operationalisation of AI Ethics. Minds and Machines 31(2): 239-256 (doi:10.1007/s11023-021-09563-w)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0004-3702(03)00135-8> , # McLaren 2003
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00382575> , # Frankel 1989
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/s11023-021-09563-w> , # Morley et al. 2021
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/s13347-024-00710-6> , # Taddeo et al. 2024
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/s43681-023-00258-9> ; # Prem 2023
    iao:0000116 "Extraction framing: a principle is a reusable ethical value, not a duty or an action, and not an actor. It carries no actor (that is the invokedBy edge) and no application target or resolution narrative (those attach to the States and the per-application literals). An abstract, open-textured value (McLaren 2003), stated at a high level and free of specific context (Prem 2023), it gains concrete meaning extensionally through the NSPE Board of Ethical Review cases that instantiate it (McLaren 2003). Provision anchoring is carried by the curated-layer proeth:nspeReference annotations and the commit-time citesProvision edges, not by the individuation of the type. Whereas a principle expresses the value a norm serves, a prohibition or boundary statement is a Constraint, not a Principle. A case yields at most one principle individual per distinct value, and distinct values remain separate individuals. Each application of the value is recorded in the appliedTo and concreteExpression lists of that individual, and the balancing and tension narratives keep per-application entries."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Refined from IAO:information content entity to IAO:directive information entity. Professional principles express the ideals stated by aspirational codes (Frankel 1989) and have been compared to constitutional principles, which provide a foundation and require interpretation in context (Morley et al. 2021; Taddeo et al. 2024). Their prescriptive character (world-to-word fit) distinguishes them from descriptive information content."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.9.0: iao:0000115 and skos:scopeNote softened so the principle type is individuated by the value it expresses alone; provision anchoring is described as the curated nspeReference layer and the commit-time citesProvision edge layer, not as type individuation. McLaren extensional grounding unchanged."@en .

# O - Obligation: Professional obligations as directive information entities (Donohue 2017)
proeth-core:Obligation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000033 ;  # IAO:directive information entity
    rdfs:label "Obligation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A directive information entity expressing required actions or behaviors in professional contexts. Obligations prescribe conduct and, when concretized by an agent, guide compliant behavior. This is the O component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A directive information entity (IAO) that specifies a required action or duty of professional conduct, prescribing behavior with a world-to-word direction of fit. An obligation is individuated by the duty it states and the Principle it derives from along the Role-Principle-Obligation chain, not by who bears it on a given occasion or whether it was met, nor by the code provision that states it; it directs a role-bearer's conduct when concretized in a case. This is the O component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The obligation type is defined by the duty it specifies and the Principle it derives from (the R->P->O chain), not by who bears it in a given case or whether it was met, nor by which code provision states it; provision anchoring is carried by the curated nspeReference annotations and the commit-time citesProvision edges, and the obligated party, compliance status, and per-occasion circumstances attach to the case individual and edges, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Dennis et al. (2016). Formal verification of ethical choices in autonomous systems. Robotics and Autonomous Systems 77: 1-14 (doi:10.1016/j.robot.2015.11.012)." ,
                "Donohue (2017). Toward a BFO-Based Deontic Ontology. Proceedings of the 2nd Workshop on Representing Social and Legal Entities in the Biomedical Domain (SoLe-BD 2017) at ICBO 2017, CEUR Workshop Proceedings 2137." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.robot.2015.11.012> , # Dennis et al. 2016
                   <https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2137/ws_SoLe_paper_1.pdf> ; # Donohue 2017 (CEUR open access)
    iao:0000116 "Extraction framing: an obligation is a specific duty a role is bound to, not the ethical value it serves (that is the Principle) nor the deed that discharges it (that is the Action). It carries no bearer, and no compliance judgment or per-occasion circumstance (those attach to the case individual via obligatedParty, complianceStatus, and temporalScope). It is materialized along the Role-Principle-Obligation chain, deriving from the Principle it operationalizes (Donohue 2017: obligations as directive information entities issued with normative authority). obligationType classifies the NSPE category of the obligation, not the deontic relation itself. A must-not or shall-not statement is a Constraint, not an Obligation. Required-ness is the differentia: guidance the source treats as recommended best practice is not an Obligation and routes to the Principle pass. A duty to comply with named requirements is a positive duty and is an Obligation even when the requirements themselves are Constraints."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Refined from IAO:information content entity to IAO:directive information entity per the Donohue (SoLe-BD 2017, at ICBO) analysis of deontic entities in BFO-aligned ontologies."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.9.0: iao:0000115 and skos:scopeNote softened so the obligation type is individuated by the duty it states and the Principle it derives from along the R->P->O chain; provision anchoring is described as the curated nspeReference layer and the commit-time citesProvision edge layer, not as type individuation."@en .

# S - State: Professional states as specifically dependent continuants
proeth-core:State a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000020 ;  # BFO:specifically dependent continuant
    rdfs:label "State"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A specifically dependent continuant representing conditions that affect ethical decisions and professional conduct. This is the S component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A specifically dependent continuant (BFO) that inheres in one or more independent-continuant bearers (an agent, a project, or an affected system) and represents a condition that affects the ethical evaluation of professional conduct. The state is individuated by the condition it represents and its persistence type, an inertial fluent that holds until an event terminates it or a non-inertial fluent true at a single time point, not by whose condition it is or when it holds, nor by which obligations it activates in a given case. This is the S component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Operationalizes the situational-context account of the Contextual Framework: a state captures the ethically relevant features of a situation (Anderson and Anderson 2018) that make otherwise identical professional actions carry different ethical weight. Each state class declares a persistence type implementing Berreby et al.'s (2017) Event Calculus distinction between inertial and non-inertial fluents, and records an urgency level carrying Jones's (1991) moral-intensity salience. The bearer subject and the activatesObligation and defeasibleUnder edges attach to the case individual, not to the class; state temporal boundaries are carried by the activatedByEvent and terminatedByEvent edges and the activePeriod literal. Reclassified from BFO quality to specifically dependent continuant (v2.3.0) because extracted states span the quality-disposition boundary, a placement that commits every state to inherence in an independent-continuant bearer. BFO provides no state-of-affairs or situation category, so situation-like conditions enter as bearer-dependent conditions rather than as freestanding situations. Stative-label rule: a state is named for the condition that holds (for example Sealed Draft Report), never for the happening that produced it. A case yields one state individual per distinct condition even when the condition is narrated in several passages. A happening-shaped candidate routes to the Event pass."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The state TYPE is defined by the condition it represents and its persistence type, an inertial fluent that holds until terminated or a non-inertial fluent true at a single time point (Event Calculus), not by whose condition it is or when it holds, nor by which obligations it activates in a given case; the bearer and the obligation-activation edges attach to the case individual, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. Proceedings of the 16th Conference on Autonomous Agents and MultiAgent Systems (AAMAS 2017): 96-104 (doi:10.65109/hhri9090)." ,
                "Jones (1991). Ethical Decision Making by Individuals in Organizations: An Issue-Contingent Model. The Academy of Management Review 16(2): 366-395 (doi:10.2307/258867)." ,
                "Anderson and Anderson (2018). GenEth: a general ethical dilemma analyzer. Paladyn, Journal of Behavioral Robotics 9(1): 337-357 (doi:10.1515/pjbr-2018-0024)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.65109/hhri9090> , # Berreby et al. 2017
                   <https://doi.org/10.2307/258867> , # Jones 1991
                   <https://doi.org/10.1515/pjbr-2018-0024> ; # Anderson and Anderson 2018
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Changed from BFO:quality to BFO:specifically dependent continuant. ProEthica States include relational and situational conditions that do not fit the strict BFO definition of quality (which requires inherence in a single bearer): states span the quality-disposition boundary, encompassing both monadic qualities of a single bearer and relational or situational conditions. SDC is the common parent of quality and realizable entity. v2.9.0: this typing rationale moved here from iao:0000115, which now carries only the genus-differentia definition."@en .

# Rs - Resource: Professional knowledge sources as information content entities
proeth-core:Resource a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030 ;  # IAO:information content entity
    rdfs:label "Resource"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An information content entity that serves as a professional knowledge source for ethical decision-making. Resources include codes of ethics, case precedents, technical standards, and expert interpretations -- all generically dependent continuants with descriptive content about professional practice. This is the Rs component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "An information content entity (IAO) that serves as a professional knowledge source consulted in the ethical evaluation of professional conduct, carrying descriptive content about professional practice with a word-to-world direction of fit. A resource is a generically dependent continuant that is not directive, which distinguishes it from the prescriptive Principle, Obligation, and Constraint components (directive information entities). The resource TYPE is individuated by its source kind, the category of professional knowledge it is (a code of ethics, a case precedent, a technical standard, a legal or regulatory resource, or reference material), not by the specific document that instances it, its topic, or who relied on or cited it in a given case; as a record of accumulated professional knowledge it gives abstract principles their extensional, precedent-based meaning. This is the Rs component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Extraction framing: a resource is a professional knowledge source consulted in a case, not a directive that governs conduct (that is P/O/Cs). It records the codes, precedents, technical standards, and interpretive guidance that give abstract principles their extensional, case-based meaning (McLaren 2003), and operationalizes Davis's (1991) account of professional codes as conventions between professionals that solve a coordination problem, and Frankel's (1989) account of the profession as a normative reference group whose code clarifies the norms that govern professional conduct. Reclassified from BFO independent continuant to IAO information content entity (v2.3.0) because all extracted resources are informational, not physical, and placed at descriptive ICE (word-to-world) without the directive refinement that distinguishes P/O/Cs. source_kind drives the five-kind subClassOf typing and is not stored as a literal. The document title, the relying actor (availableTo), and the citing authority (citedByAgent) attach to the case individual and its edges, not to the class, while governedByCode is a class-level TBox restriction relevant only to code resources."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The resource TYPE (class) is a bare category of professional knowledge, a kind of law, regulation, standard, or code, while a specific, named or numbered, separately citable document or data artifact present in the case (a code provision, a precedent case, a dataset) is an INDIVIDUAL; the relying actor and the citing authority attach to the individual and its edges (availableTo, citedByAgent), not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "McLaren (2003). Extensionally defining principles and cases in ethics: an AI model. Artificial Intelligence 150(1-2): 145-181 (doi:10.1016/S0004-3702(03)00135-8)." ,
                "Davis (1991). Thinking like an engineer: the place of a code of ethics in the practice of a profession. Philosophy & Public Affairs 20(2): 150-167." ,
                "Frankel (1989). Professional codes: why, how, and with what impact? Journal of Business Ethics 8(2-3): 109-115 (doi:10.1007/BF00382575)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0004-3702(03)00135-8> , # McLaren 2003
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00382575> , # Frankel 1989
                   <https://www.jstor.org/stable/2265293> ; # Davis 1991 (pre-DOI era)
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Changed from BFO:independent continuant to IAO:information content entity. All extracted resources (codes, precedents, standards, interpretations) are generically dependent continuants, not independent continuants. Resources are descriptive ICEs (they describe existing professional knowledge) as distinct from the prescriptive ICEs (P, O, Cs) that direct conduct."@en .

# A - Action: Professional actions as processes
proeth-core:Action a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000015 ;  # BFO:process
    rdfs:label "Action"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A volitional professional intervention an agent performs through deliberate choice, modeled as a BFO process. This is the A component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A process (BFO) that is a volitional, intentional intervention an agent performs through deliberate choice in a professional context, the temporal-dynamics counterpart of the Event. Whereas an Event is a non-volitional occurrence that happens to or around the agents in a case, an Action issues from an agent's deliberate choice and is therefore the occurrent to which professional responsibility, and the fulfillment or breach of obligations, attaches. As a happening in the Event-Calculus sense, an Action initiates and terminates the State fluents that carry the situation forward and stands as a cause in the case's causal chain, so responsibility for a downstream outcome can be attributed to it. The action TYPE is individuated by the kind of volitional intervention it is, not by who performs it, when it occurs, or the specific States it initiates in a given case; because actions are the particular occurrences of a single case timeline they are recorded as individuals of this class rather than through per-case subclasses. This is the A component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Operationalizes the volitional-intervention account of the Temporal Dynamics dimension. An action is a deliberate professional choice subject to ethical evaluation (an action follows from a volition, Sarmiento et al. 2022; intention is first-class in the Govindarajulu and Bringsjord 2017 double-effect formalization; agent-initiated action versus automatic event, Berreby et al. 2017), separated from the Event precisely to support a factual causal inquiry that grounds later responsibility attribution: no blame or praise may be assigned without an account of the causal relationship between agent and outcome (Sarmiento et al. 2022, quoting Beebee et al. 2009); the factual inquiry uses the cause-in-fact analysis that the NESS test of Wright (1985) makes precise and that Sarmiento et al. formalize for action languages; causality is one of the three normative bases of accountability alongside fault and duty (Kroll 2020); and accountability separates from responsibility, which requires the intentionality that only volitional action carries (Floridi and Sanders 2004). Extraction discipline for telling Action from Event: type a happening as an Action when the text emphasizes decision, choice, intention, or deliberation by an agent. Redirect a happening that emphasizes occurrence, trigger, or consequence without agent volition to the Event pass. A single scenario routinely contains both the volitional decision and its downstream occurrence (for example 'the engineer decides to report' is the Action, 'the report is filed' is the resulting AgentCausedEvent): extract the Action for the volitional choice and let the Event pass capture the occurrence. Step 3 emits actions as individuals typed directly to core:Action with no per-case subclass discovery. Each is anchored by time:hasTime (a time:Instant or time:ProperInterval) and reified Allen interval relations, records its State transitions through the initiates and terminates fluent edges (Kowalski and Sergot 1986), and carries its normative edges (fulfillsObligation, violatesObligation, guidedByPrinciple, raisesObligation) on the case individual. An omission, a required or expected act not performed, is extractable as an Action when the case treats the non-performance as the agent's conduct."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The action TYPE is defined by the kind of volitional intervention it is (a BFO process the agent performs), not by who performs it, when, or the specific States (fluents) it initiates in a given case; the actor, the OWL-Time temporal anchor (time:hasTime) and the reified Allen interval relations, and the proeth-core:initiates edges to States attach to the case individual, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Sarmiento et al. (2022). Action languages based actual causality in decision making contexts. PRIMA 2022, LNCS 13753: 243-259 (doi:10.1007/978-3-031-21203-1_15)." ,
                "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. AAMAS 2017." ,
                "Wright (1985). Causation in tort law. California Law Review 73: 1735-1828 (doi:10.2307/3480373)." ,
                "Kroll (2020). Accountability in computer systems. The Oxford Handbook of Ethics of AI: 180-196 (doi:10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190067397.013.10)." ,
                "Floridi and Sanders (2004). On the morality of artificial agents. Minds and Machines 14(3): 349-379 (doi:10.1023/b:mind.0000035461.63578.9d)." ,
                "Govindarajulu and Bringsjord (2017). On automating the doctrine of double effect. IJCAI 2017: 4722-4730 (doi:10.24963/ijcai.2017/658)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-21203-1_15> , # Sarmiento et al. 2022
                   <https://doi.org/10.2307/3480373> , # Wright 1985
                   <https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190067397.013.10> , # Kroll 2020
                   <https://doi.org/10.1023/b:mind.0000035461.63578.9d> , # Floridi and Sanders 2004
                   <https://doi.org/10.24963/ijcai.2017/658> . # Govindarajulu and Bringsjord 2017

# E - Event: Professional events as processes  
proeth-core:Event a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000015 ;  # BFO:process
    rdfs:label "Event"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A non-volitional occurrence in a professional context, individuated by its origin (agent-caused, exogenous, or automatic), modeled as a BFO process. This is the E component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A process (BFO) that occurs within a professional case timeline as a non-volitional occurrence, an occurrence not carried out as the deliberate intervention of an agent, that changes the situational state of the case by initiating or terminating the State fluents on which obligations depend. Whereas an Action is a volitional intervention the agent performs and for which the agent bears professional responsibility, an Event happens to the case, so the boundary separating the two is agency rather than temporal structure: both are BFO processes, and an instantaneous event is typed as a process for uniformity with durative events. An Event is individuated by its origin, one of three mutually disjoint kinds: agent-caused (a downstream consequence of an agent's action), exogenous (no proximate agent cause, originating outside the agents in the case), or automatic (following from a system or process operating under set rules once its preconditions, represented as fluents, hold without direct agent input). An Event is not individuated by when it happens, its duration, or the specific States it initiates or terminates in a given case. This is the E component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Operationalizes Berreby et al.'s (2017) Event Calculus distinction between agent-caused actions and automatic events. An automatic event fires when all of its preconditions, represented as fluents, hold without direct input from the agent (Berreby et al. 2017). Kowalski and Sergot's (1986) event calculus supplies the canonical initiates and terminates fluent-transition semantics that the initiates and terminates edges implement, connecting the temporal components to the normative ones (Event initiates State, State activatesObligation). The three origin subclasses realize Berreby's exogenous-versus-automatic contrast together with the exogenous-event separation of Sarmiento et al. (2022), who set events (their vocabulary connotes agentless actions, quoted from Russell and Norvig) apart from actions that follow from a volition, for factual causal inquiry and later responsibility attribution. The wearable-robot case of Almpani et al. (2023), in which an emergency activates a contingency protocol that changes which ethical policy applies, is why Events must be captured: an exogenous emergency event can modify the constraint environment at decision time. Extraction discipline (Step 3, the LangGraph temporal pass over combined Facts and Discussion): type an occurrence as an Event when it arises without an actor's deliberate choice, and redirect any volitional professional intervention to the Action pass. The operative test is agency, not whether the happening is instantaneous or durative and not whether it is momentous. event_type is the routing input that drives the three-way subClassOf typing (outcome to AgentCausedEvent, exogenous to ExogenousEvent, automatic to AutomaticEvent) and is not stored as a literal. Events are emitted as case individuals typed to core:Event with no per-case subclass discovery, because they are the particular occurrences of a single case timeline rather than reusable vocabulary. The OWL-Time anchor (time:hasTime), the reified Allen interval relations, the initiates and terminates fluent edges to States, and the causedByAction link to the enabling Action all attach to the case individual, not to the class. Origin tie-break: when the proximate producer of a happening is a system executing set rules, type it AutomaticEvent even if an agent action supplied the preconditions, and record that enabling action via the causedByAction link. AgentCausedEvent is reserved for consequences not mediated by automated rule-following."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The event TYPE is defined by the kind of non-volitional occurrence it is and its origin (agent-caused, exogenous, or automatic), not by when it happens or the specific States (fluents) it initiates or terminates in a given case; the OWL-Time temporal anchor (time:hasTime) and the reified Allen interval relations, and the proeth-core:initiates / proeth-core:terminates edges to States attach to the case individual, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. AAMAS 2017." ,
                "Kowalski and Sergot (1986). A logic-based calculus of events. New Generation Computing 4(1): 67-95 (doi:10.1007/BF03037383)." ,
                "Sarmiento et al. (2022). Action languages based actual causality in decision making contexts. PRIMA 2022, LNCS 13753: 243-259 (doi:10.1007/978-3-031-21203-1_15)." ,
                "Almpani et al. (2023). Formalization of ethical decision making: implementation in the data privacy of wearable robots. International Journal of Extreme Automation and Connectivity in Healthcare 5(1): 1-13 (doi:10.4018/ijeach.320488)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF03037383> , # Kowalski and Sergot 1986
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-21203-1_15> , # Sarmiento et al. 2022
                   <https://doi.org/10.4018/ijeach.320488> . # Almpani et al. 2023

# Event origin axis (E3/E4, ratified): three mutually-disjoint origin subclasses grounding the Action/Event
# split (Berreby et al. 2017 exogenous-vs-automatic; Sarmiento responsibility attribution). ONT-4 (2026-07-01):
# the topical Action/Event taxonomies in intermediate are now owl:deprecated (an earlier version of this comment
# wrongly claimed they were already deleted), and the ProEthica commit bridge maps the emitted eventType
# (outcome/exogenous/automatic) to these three origin subclasses; Action is bare (no subtype) per the spec.
proeth-core:AgentCausedEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf proeth-core:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Agent-Caused Event"@en ;
    iao:0000115 "An event occurring as a downstream consequence of an agent's action."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An event occurring as a downstream consequence of an agent's action."@en ;
    skos:definition "An event occurring as a downstream consequence of an agent's action."@en .

proeth-core:ExogenousEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf proeth-core:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Exogenous Event"@en ;
    iao:0000115 "An event with no proximate agent cause, originating outside the agents in the case."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An event with no proximate agent cause, originating outside the agents in the case."@en ;
    skos:definition "An event with no proximate agent cause, originating outside the agents in the case."@en .

proeth-core:AutomaticEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf proeth-core:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Automatic Event"@en ;
    iao:0000115 "An event following automatically from a system or process operating under set rules. When the proximate producer of a happening is such a system, it is typed AutomaticEvent even if an agent action supplied the preconditions (the enabling action is recorded via the causedByAction link); AgentCausedEvent is reserved for consequences not mediated by automated rule-following."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An event following automatically from a system or process operating under set rules. When the proximate producer of a happening is such a system, it is typed AutomaticEvent even if an agent action supplied the preconditions (the enabling action is recorded via the causedByAction link); AgentCausedEvent is reserved for consequences not mediated by automated rule-following."@en ;
    skos:definition "An event following automatically from a system or process operating under set rules. When the proximate producer of a happening is such a system, it is typed AutomaticEvent even if an agent action supplied the preconditions (the enabling action is recorded via the causedByAction link); AgentCausedEvent is reserved for consequences not mediated by automated rule-following."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
    owl:members ( proeth-core:AgentCausedEvent proeth-core:ExogenousEvent proeth-core:AutomaticEvent ) .

# Ca - Capability: Professional capabilities as dispositions
proeth-core:Capability a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000016 ;  # BFO:disposition
    rdfs:label "Capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A disposition that inheres in a professional agent and is realized through professional activities. Capabilities represent skills, competencies, and abilities that exist as potentials and are realized when the agent performs corresponding actions. This is the Ca component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A disposition (BFO) that inheres in a professional agent and is realized through the professional activities that exercise it, representing a competence the agent possesses. Capabilities span a range from the recognition-and-reasoning competencies (perceiving a situation, knowing which norms apply and resolving their conflict, tracking evolving standards, and explaining and justifying decisions) to the enacted competence to devise and carry through a response to an ethical problem treated as a design problem. A capability is individuated by the competence it is, the canonical kind of disposition, not by who possesses it on a given occasion, the proficiency shown, or which obligations presuppose it in a given case; it exists as a potential and is realized when the agent performs the corresponding professional actions. This is the Ca component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Operationalizes the professional-competence account of the framework. The recognition-and-reasoning competencies are structured by four ethical-competence requirements drawn, in the analysis grounding this framework, from the Tolmeijer et al. (2021) survey of machine-ethics implementations (the four labels are the analysis vocabulary, not the survey's own), named per kind by the tolmeijerRequirement annotation: norm competence, situational awareness, learning and adaptation, and explanation and justification. The enacted competence to devise and carry through a response is framed by Epstein and Hundert's (2002) account of professional competence as the judicious use of knowledge, skill, reasoning, and reflection in practice. The competence kind is carried as the candidate class label and resolved by the match decision to a named capability class; minted novel kinds anchor at the bare Capability class (no separate capability_kind schema field exists). tolmeijerRequirement is a class-level annotation naming which requirement the kind primarily realizes, with no per-individual field and no second typing axiom. Only a competence the agent actually possesses is typed here. A lacked or unexercised competence is not typed as a Capability: it reaches the case as a CompetenceGapState. The possessor (possessedBy) and the incoming Obligation-to-Capability requiresCapability edges, encoding that an obligation presupposes the capacity to discharge it, attach to the case individual, not to the class."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The capability TYPE is defined by the competence it is, a BFO disposition realized through professional activity, not by who possesses it in a given case or the specific obligations it enables there; the bearer and the incoming requiresCapability edges (obligation presupposes capacity) attach to the case individual, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Tolmeijer et al. (2021). Implementations in machine ethics: a survey. ACM Computing Surveys 53(6) (doi:10.1145/3419633)." ,
                "Epstein and Hundert (2002). Defining and assessing professional competence. JAMA 287(2): 226-235 (doi:10.1001/jama.287.2.226)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1145/3419633> , # Tolmeijer et al. 2021
                   <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.287.2.226> ; # Epstein and Hundert 2002
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Reverted from BFO:realizable entity to BFO:disposition. Extracted capabilities (technical skills, ethical reasoning abilities, communication competencies) are dispositions -- they inhere in individual bearers and are realized through professional processes. Disposition is the more precise subclass of realizable entity."@en .

# Cs - Constraint: Professional constraints as directive information entities
proeth-core:Constraint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000033 ;  # IAO:directive information entity
    rdfs:label "Constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A directive information entity expressing limitations or restrictions on professional actions or decisions. Constraints prescribe boundaries on permissible conduct. This is the Cs component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A directive information entity (IAO) that prescribes a boundary on professional conduct by specifying a prohibited action, or the outer limit of the permissible-action space, that a bearer of the relevant role must not cross. Whereas an Obligation prescribes an action that is required and a Principle expresses an abstract value or aim, a Constraint carries the prohibition/boundary deontic modality, foreclosing a region of action-space rather than mandating an act or orienting toward a good. A Constraint is established by a source provision and ranges in force from an inviolable prohibition to a defeasible default that tolerates justified exceptions under specified circumstances. This is the Cs component of the formal specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs)."@en ;
    iao:0000116 "The Constraint type (Cs) is individuated by the boundary it states and its source provision, not by the party it binds in a given case or by how a defeasible exception is resolved. Grounded in the boundary literature: constraints establish the negative behavioral limits that determine the permissible action space (Arkin 2008; Arkin et al. 2012, where prohibition constraints are evaluated first and a failing constraint suppresses the behavior), they are specified as prohibited actions under temporal and contextual conditions, the specification of abstract principles into precise context-dependent constraints (Dennis et al. 2016), and they operate as default rules that retain a general prohibition while tolerating justified exceptions in extraordinary circumstances (Ganascia 2007), and their formal verification is enabled by deontic-logic encodings of normative theories (Benzmüller et al. 2020). Extraction discipline: type a 'must not / may not / is barred from / is prohibited from' statement, or a statement of an outer limit on the action space, as a Constraint. Redirect any positive 'was required to / shall / must do' statement to the Obligation pass and any abstract value or aim to the Principle pass. boundary_type is a routing input that drives the subClassOf typing into one of the ten closed boundary-type classes and is not stored as a literal. severity is retained as a genuine attribute (Dennis severity ordering). The intrinsic hard/soft/negotiable flexibility of a single constraint is distinct from the extrinsic competition between two obligations, which is carried by the defeasibility object properties declared on Obligation, not here. A provision the code states negatively (shall not X) is extracted once, as a Constraint. The Obligation pass records only a separately stated positive duty and cross-references the constraint rather than restating the prohibition."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The constraint TYPE is defined by the prohibition or boundary it states and its source provision, not by who it binds in a given case or how a defeasible exception is resolved; the constrained party, per-occasion applicability, and any exception narrative attach to the case individual and edges, not to the class."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Arkin (2008). Governing lethal behavior: embedding ethics in a hybrid deliberative/reactive robot architecture. HRI 2008: 121-128 (doi:10.1145/1349822.1349839)." ,
                "Ganascia (2007). Modelling ethical rules of lying with Answer Set Programming. Ethics and Information Technology 9(1): 39-47 (doi:10.1007/s10676-006-9134-y)." ,
                "Dennis et al. (2016). Formal verification of ethical choices in autonomous systems. Robotics and Autonomous Systems 77: 1-14 (doi:10.1016/j.robot.2015.11.012)." ,
                "Benzmüller et al. (2020). Designing normative theories for ethical and legal reasoning: LogiKEy framework, methodology, and tool support. Artificial Intelligence 287: 103348 (doi:10.1016/j.artint.2020.103348)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1145/1349822.1349839> , # Arkin 2008
                   <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10676-006-9134-y> , # Ganascia 2007
                   <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.robot.2015.11.012> , # Dennis et al. 2016
                   <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.artint.2020.103348> ; # Benzmueller et al. 2020
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Refined from IAO:information content entity to IAO:directive information entity. Constraints define the permissible space of action through negative behavioral limits (Arkin 2008) and prohibitions specifying what must not be done (Ganascia 2007). Their specification of prohibited actions under temporal and contextual conditions (Dennis et al. 2016) constitutes directive content."@en .

###############################################################
# CodeProvision: Extension for Ethics Code Integration
# Added: 2026-01-12 - Support for representing guideline provisions
###############################################################

# CodeProvision: Individual provisions from codes of ethics
proeth-core:CodeProvision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030 ;  # IAO:information content entity
    rdfs:label "Code Provision"@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A discrete normative statement from a code of ethics that establishes ethical requirements for professional conduct."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An information content entity representing a single provision, section, or rule from a professional code of ethics (e.g., NSPE Code of Ethics, NEA Code of Ethics). Provisions establish the normative foundation from which Principles, Obligations, and Constraints are derived."@en ;
    skos:definition "A discrete normative statement from a code of ethics that establishes ethical requirements for professional conduct."@en ;
    skos:example "NSPE Code II.1.c: 'Engineers shall not reveal facts, data, or information without the prior consent of the client or employer except as authorized or required by law or this Code.'"@en ;
    iao:0000116 "Boundary with Resource (deliberate, mirrors the v2.7.3 Guideline decision from the other side): a CodeProvision is the normative STATEMENT itself, a directive information entity like Principle/Obligation/Constraint, and is therefore owl:disjointWith Resource. The citable knowledge SOURCE is its containing document, the Guideline/EthicalCode, which IS a Resource. The two are linked by partOfGuideline / containsProvision. Typing a provision as a Resource would double-count the citation (document and statement) and cross the directive/descriptive line the Resource definition draws."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "CodeProvisions serve as the authoritative source for derived ethical concepts. When analyzing cases, provisions are cited to justify the application of principles and obligations."@en .

# Guideline: Container for provisions (the full code document)
proeth-core:Guideline a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000310 ;  # IAO:document (IAO_0000310 = "document"; codes of ethics are documents)
    rdfs:subClassOf proeth-core:Resource ;  # a guideline document IS a professional knowledge source (Rs); both are IAO ICEs (v2.7.3)
    rdfs:label "Guideline"@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A formal document establishing ethical standards for a profession, containing individual CodeProvisions."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A professional code of ethics or guideline document containing multiple provisions. Examples include NSPE Code of Ethics, NEA Code of Ethics, ACM Code of Ethics."@en ;
    skos:definition "A formal document establishing ethical standards for a profession, containing individual CodeProvisions."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.7.3: Added rdfs:subClassOf proeth-core:Resource and removed the former Guideline owl:disjointWith Resource axiom. A guideline document (e.g. an ethical code) is itself a professional knowledge source (the Rs component); both Guideline and Resource are IAO information content entities, so the subsumption is BFO-sound. This lets EthicalCode -- both a Guideline (document) and the Resource cases cite -- be typed consistently, fixing the unsatisfiability that propagated to every ProfessionalRole via 'governedByCode some EthicalCode'."@en .

# Agent: Supporting class for role bearers
proeth-core:Agent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:0000040 ;  # BFO:material entity
    rdfs:label "Agent"@en ;
    iao:0000115 "A material entity capable of bearing roles and performing intentional actions in professional contexts."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A material entity capable of bearing roles and performing intentional actions in professional contexts."@en ;
    skos:definition "A material entity capable of bearing roles and performing intentional actions in professional contexts."@en .

###############################################################
# Nine-component D-tuple framework membership
###############################################################
# Which slot each component class fills (R, P, O, ...) is metadata about the representational
# framework, not a domain fact, so it is carried by ANNOTATION properties. componentOf links a
# component class to the framework individual via an annotation property, NOT an object property,
# so a class is never asserted to be a logical member of an individual (no class-as-member punning).
# The mutually-disjoint structure of the nine is already formalized by the owl:AllDisjointClasses below.

proeth-core:dtupleComponent a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "D-tuple component symbol"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The symbol (R, P, O, S, Rs, A, E, Ca, Cs) this class fills in the D-tuple framework."@en .
proeth-core:componentOf a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "component of"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a component class to the representational framework it is a component of. An annotation property (not owl:ObjectProperty / part-of) to avoid asserting a class as a member of an individual."@en .

# The framework as an information artifact (the formal specification this ontology implements). This is
# the single foundational meta-individual in core; domain particulars are still recorded in the case files, so the
# TBox/ABox separation holds for the domain layer.
proeth-core:DTupleFramework a owl:NamedIndividual, iao:0000030 ;  # IAO:information content entity
    rdfs:label "Nine-component D-tuple framework"@en ;
    skos:definition "The formal nine-component specification D=(R,P,O,S,Rs,A,E,Ca,Cs) that this ontology implements: the contextual components (Role, State, Resource), the normative components (Principle, Obligation, Capability, Constraint), and the temporal components (Action, Event). An information content entity: a formal representational specification."@en .

proeth-core:Role        proeth-core:dtupleComponent "R"  ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Principle   proeth-core:dtupleComponent "P"  ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Obligation  proeth-core:dtupleComponent "O"  ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:State       proeth-core:dtupleComponent "S"  ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Resource    proeth-core:dtupleComponent "Rs" ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Action      proeth-core:dtupleComponent "A"  ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Event       proeth-core:dtupleComponent "E"  ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Capability  proeth-core:dtupleComponent "Ca" ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .
proeth-core:Constraint  proeth-core:dtupleComponent "Cs" ; proeth-core:componentOf proeth-core:DTupleFramework .

###############################################################
# Core Object Properties 
###############################################################

proeth-core:hasRole a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ro:0000087 ;  # RO:has role
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:label "has role"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an agent to a role it bears."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an agent to a role it bears."@en .

proeth-core:isRoleOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf proeth-core:hasRole ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ro:0000081 ;  # RO:role of
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "is role of"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a role to the agent that bears it. Inverse of hasRole, so the reasoner sees both directions from one asserted edge. Grounded under RO:role of."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Role to the Agent who bears it. Inverse of hasRole."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.7.2: grounded rdfs:subPropertyOf ro:role_of."@en .

proeth-core:hasObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "has obligation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a role to an obligation borne in that standing. The Role-wide domain enables the edge for any role without committing existence; obligation-generation is axiomatized on proeth:ProfessionalRole (subClassOf hasObligation some Obligation) in the intermediate layer, and a participant role may bear case-asserted obligations without profession-generated ones."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a role to an obligation that directs conduct in that standing."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.5: comment universalized; the former wording (its professional obligations) predated the v2.10.0 Role universalization and contradicted committed participant-side edges."@en .

proeth-core:adheresToPrinciple a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Principle ;
    rdfs:label "adheres to principle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a role to principles that guide its conduct; the role-relative applicability edge of the R->P->O chain (the role determines which principles apply to conduct in that standing)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a role to principles that guide its conduct."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Removed subPropertyOf iao:0000136 (is about). The is_about relation links information content to what it represents; adheresToPrinciple links a role to the directive content that governs it, which is a different semantic relation."@en .

# --- IAO annotation properties: definition provenance + editor framing (declared locally so the
# alignment is self-contained when the validation harness strips owl:imports). ---
iao:0000119 a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "definition source"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The literature source(s) a class definition is grounded in."@en .
iao:0000116 a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "editor note"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Editor note; used here to carry the literature-grounded extraction framing for a component."@en .

# --- Role-type definitional attributes (the R component's class-level schema, Fix 2B).
# Annotation properties applied to role CLASSES (the role-type carries them), distinct from the
# per-bearer individual data (license, specialty, ...) which attach to role individuals. The
# extraction prompt's class attributes derive from these (hybrid: ontology owns the definition). ---
proeth-core:distinguishingFeatures a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "distinguishing features"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "What sets this role type apart from related roles (the distinctive ends/practices that individuate the role)."@en .
proeth-core:professionalScope a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "professional scope"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The areas of responsibility and authority of the role type (Oakley and Cocking 2001)."@en .
proeth-core:typicalQualifications a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "typical qualifications"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Generic credentials the role TYPE requires (a descriptor of the type, e.g. 'Professional Engineer license' as a requirement, NOT an individual's license value)."@en .
proeth-core:associatedVirtues a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "associated virtues"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Virtues and dispositions characteristic of the role type (Kong et al. 2020 identity virtues; Oakley and Cocking 2001). A literal for now; may be promoted to edges toward a Virtue class."@en .

proeth-core:hasCapability a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "has capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an agent to capabilities they possess. The committed corpus asserts the possessedBy direction (the participant edge family); hasCapability is the reasoner-derived Agent-side reading, retained as declared query vocabulary."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an agent to capabilities they possess."@en .

proeth-core:performsAction a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ro:0000056 ;  # RO:participates in (a continuant participates in a process)
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:label "performs action"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an agent to actions they perform."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an agent to actions they perform."@en .

proeth-core:isPerformedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf proeth-core:performsAction ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ro:0000057 ;  # RO:has participant (a process has a continuant participant)
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "is performed by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to the agent that performs it. Inverse of performsAction."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an action to the agent responsible for performing it."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.4.1: Added as named inverse of performsAction to support OWL DL-compliant cardinality restriction on Action."@en .

proeth-core:fulfillsObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "fulfills obligation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to obligations it fulfills. The action realizes the directive content of the obligation."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Action to an Obligation it fulfills."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.3.0: Removed subPropertyOf ro:0000057 (has participant). RO:has_participant relates a process to a continuant participant; an obligation is the directive content the action realizes (a generically dependent continuant), not a participant involved in the action in the BFO sense."@en .

proeth-core:realizesCapability a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "realizes capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to capabilities it realizes."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an action to capabilities it realizes."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-07: reserved with the Action-side enrichment family (usesResource pattern) rather than deprecated; unused in committed data, where the Action-side signal is carried by the requiresCapabilityText literal."@en .

proeth-core:hasState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:label "has state"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to contextual states that affect its evaluation. An intentional pragmatic deviation from strict BFO inherence (specifically dependent continuants inhere in independent continuants, not processes): it links a process to contextual conditions, such as emergency conditions or competence states, without reifying the SDC-bearer-process chain. Deprecated: no extractor or committed case ever emitted the edge; the live design connects states to happenings via the fluent family (initiates/terminates, activatedByEvent/terminatedByEvent) and to agents via affects."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an action to contextual states that affect its evaluation."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.4.2: Documented as intentional pragmatic deviation from strict BFO. The domain (Action/process) to range (State/SDC) link is retained because the alternative (reifying the independent continuant bearer for each state) would add modeling complexity disproportionate to the extraction use case."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-06: marked owl:deprecated; unused in all committed cases. The v2.4.2 rationale is preserved above should a manual-annotation use revive it."@en .

proeth-core:usesResource a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "uses resource"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to resources it utilizes. Reserved as the canonical Action-to-Resource edge for the Step-4 enrichment vocabulary; not yet written by the extraction pipeline."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-07: the surviving Action-to-Resource property after the hasResource deletion; unused until the Step-4 enrichment vocabulary is populated. Do not deprecate on the zero-use test."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an action to resources it utilizes."@en .

proeth-core:triggersEvent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ro:0002411 ;  # RO:causally upstream of (RO_0002411)
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Event ;
    rdfs:label "triggers event"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to events it causes or initiates. Deprecated: no emitter or consumer ever existed and no committed case carries it; the causal Event-to-Action link is carried by proeth:causedByAction (the semantic inverse, intermediate namespace), the NESS analysis by the CausalChain family, and the ordering by the Allen/TemporalRelation machinery."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an action to events it causes or initiates."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-07: marked owl:deprecated (unused in all committed cases; the hasState precedent). The ro:0002411 grounding note is preserved below."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.4.1: Corrected RO IRI from non-existent ro:0000012 to ro:0002411 (causally upstream of). RO_0002411 is the correct IRI for 'causally upstream of' in the OBO Relations Ontology."@en .

proeth-core:constrainedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "constrained by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to constraints that limit or restrict it. Reserved with the Action-side enrichment vocabulary; not yet written by the extraction pipeline."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-07: reserved with the Action-side enrichment family (usesResource pattern) rather than deprecated; unused in committed data. Do not deprecate on the zero-use test."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an action to constraints that limit or restrict it."@en .

###############################################################
# Defeasibility Relations (v2.5.0)
# Added: 2026-04-11 - Structural support for obligation competition
#
# These three properties expose the defeasibility pattern described in the
# D-tuple formalism as first-class object properties between typed
# individuals, rather than leaving the pattern implicit in class names or
# narrative datatype fields. They do NOT encode non-monotonic inference
# (OWL-DL cannot); they make the competing-obligation structure queryable
# via SPARQL and available to external non-monotonic engines.
#
# Usage guidance:
#   O1  proeth-core:competesWith     O2   (symmetric; two obligations in tension)
#   O2  proeth-core:prevailsOver     O1   (directed; winner under defeat conditions)
#   O1  proeth-core:defeasibleUnder  S    (obligation yields when state S obtains)
###############################################################

proeth-core:competesWith a owl:ObjectProperty, owl:SymmetricProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "competes with"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an obligation to another obligation with which it stands in normative tension within a case. Symmetric: if O1 competes with O2 then O2 competes with O1. Does not itself specify which obligation prevails; use prevailsOver for the directed resolution."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links two Obligations that are in direct normative competition within a case scenario."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typical pattern: an obligation derived from one Principle competes with an obligation derived from another Principle of the same professional code, or with an obligation that inherits from a role-level constraint. The competition structure follows the prima facie duty account: several duties can bear on one situation, each binding unless overridden by another in the circumstances (Ross and Stratton-Lake 2007); Anderson and Anderson (2011) formalize prima facie duties computationally."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Ross and Stratton-Lake (2007). The Right and the Good. Clarendon Press." ,
                "Anderson and Anderson (2011). A Prima Facie Duty Approach to Machine Ethics: Machine Learning of Features of Ethical Dilemmas, Prima Facie Duties, and Decision Principles through a Dialogue with Ethicists. In Machine Ethics. Cambridge University Press: 476-492 (doi:10.1017/CBO9780511978036.032)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511978036.032> . # Anderson and Anderson 2011

proeth-core:prevailsOver a owl:ObjectProperty, owl:AsymmetricProperty, owl:IrreflexiveProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "prevails over"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an obligation to another obligation that it defeats under the conditions of the case. The prevailing obligation retains its force; the defeated obligation is subordinated to it. Use together with defeasibleUnder to record the State that licenses the resolution."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a prevailing Obligation to a defeated Obligation within a specific case resolution."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "prevailsOver is not a logical override relation in OWL-DL. It records a case-specific resolution of an obligation competition; generalizing across cases is the job of an external non-monotonic engine. The directed resolution mirrors the prima facie account: the more stringent duty becomes the duty proper in the circumstances (Ross and Stratton-Lake 2007), and Anderson and Anderson (2011) learn decision principles that select the overriding duty."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-05-23: owl:AsymmetricProperty and owl:IrreflexiveProperty asserted so the reasoner checks the edge layer, not only the type layer (a bidirectional-edge artifact in committed data motivated the hardening). The properties themselves date to v2.5.0."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Ross and Stratton-Lake (2007). The Right and the Good. Clarendon Press." ,
                "Anderson and Anderson (2011). A Prima Facie Duty Approach to Machine Ethics: Machine Learning of Features of Ethical Dilemmas, Prima Facie Duties, and Decision Principles through a Dialogue with Ethicists. In Machine Ethics. Cambridge University Press: 476-492 (doi:10.1017/CBO9780511978036.032)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511978036.032> . # Anderson and Anderson 2011

proeth-core:defeasibleUnder a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:label "defeasible under"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an obligation to a State whose obtaining renders the obligation defeasible, that is, subject to override by a competing obligation with stronger normative support. The State specifies the context in which the obligation yields."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links an Obligation to a State whose obtaining activates the obligation's defeasibility."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typical pattern: an obligation that normally holds becomes defeasible when a State representing risk or unrealizability obtains. The circumstance-dependence follows the prima facie duty account: whether a prima facie duty becomes the duty proper depends on the circumstances that obtain (Ross and Stratton-Lake 2007). The unrealizability arm adapts Kant's ought-implies-can principle, whose computational analogue Stenseke (2024) develops: an obligation lapses when the agent lacks the capacity to discharge it. As a design extension, an Obligation is also defeasible when fulfilling it would cause a harm the role exists to prevent."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Ross and Stratton-Lake (2007). The Right and the Good. Clarendon Press." .

###############################################################
# State-anchored structural properties (2026-05-30)
# Materialize the state-extraction linkages that drive the methodology:
#   S activatesObligation O   (a state makes an obligation applicable; the
#                              forward complement of defeasibleUnder's yielding)
#   S activatesConstraint Cs  (a state activates an action constraint)
#   S activatedByEvent E      (Event Calculus initiation: the event that brings
#                              the state into being)
#   S terminatedByEvent E     (Event Calculus termination: the event that ends it)
# Each is materialized at commit from the extracted state fields and carries a
# prov:Derivation, so the role of the state in obligation and constraint activation and
# its temporal boundaries are first-class and SPARQL-queryable.
###############################################################

proeth-core:activatesObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "activates obligation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a State to an Obligation that the obtaining of the State makes applicable. This is the forward, activating complement of defeasibleUnder (which records the State under which an obligation yields): a state such as hazard exposure or an undertaken commitment can bring an obligation into force for the role-bearers it concerns."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a State to an Obligation that the State's obtaining renders applicable."@en .

proeth-core:activatesConstraint a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "activates constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a State to a Constraint that the obtaining of the State activates, bounding the actions available to the role-bearers the state concerns (the S->Cs linkage)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a State to a Constraint that the State's obtaining activates."@en .

proeth-core:activatedByEvent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf [ owl:inverseOf proeth-core:initiates ] ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Event ;
    rdfs:label "activated by event"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Event Calculus initiation: relates a State to the Event whose occurrence brings the State into being (the start boundary of the fluent)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a State to the Event that initiates it."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-06: rdfs:subPropertyOf the inverse of initiates asserted, so the converse reading documented at v2.7.0 is reasoner-visible; the two families remain independently populated evidence channels with no materialized closure."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Kowalski and Sergot (1986). A logic-based calculus of events. New Generation Computing 4(1): 67-95 (doi:10.1007/BF03037383)." ,
                "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. Proceedings of the 16th Conference on Autonomous Agents and MultiAgent Systems (AAMAS 2017): 96-104 (doi:10.65109/hhri9090)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF03037383> , # Kowalski and Sergot 1986
                   <https://doi.org/10.65109/hhri9090> . # Berreby et al. 2017

proeth-core:terminatedByEvent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf [ owl:inverseOf proeth-core:terminates ] ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Event ;
    rdfs:label "terminated by event"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Event Calculus termination: relates a State to the Event whose occurrence ends the State (the end boundary of the fluent)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a State to the Event that terminates it."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-06: rdfs:subPropertyOf the inverse of terminates asserted, mirroring activatedByEvent."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Kowalski and Sergot (1986). A logic-based calculus of events. New Generation Computing 4(1): 67-95 (doi:10.1007/BF03037383)." ,
                "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. Proceedings of the 16th Conference on Autonomous Agents and MultiAgent Systems (AAMAS 2017): 96-104 (doi:10.65109/hhri9090)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF03037383> , # Kowalski and Sergot 1986
                   <https://doi.org/10.65109/hhri9090> . # Berreby et al. 2017

# Fluent transitions (v2.7.0). The canonical Event Calculus direction: an event
# initiates or terminates what holds (Kowalski and Sergot 1986, whose Initiates and
# Terminates predicates range over relationships; Berreby et al. 2017 carry the same
# machinery over fluents). Here a happening, the umbrella term of this design for an
# Action (volitional) or an Event (non-volitional), initiates or terminates a State
# (fluent). These generalize the State-side activatedByEvent / terminatedByEvent (which
# cover Events only, from the State side) to both happening kinds in the happening ->
# State direction, and the converse reading is axiomatized (activatedByEvent is a
# subproperty of the inverse of initiates, likewise the terminated pair), so reasoned
# queries see one family even though the two are populated by independent evidence
# channels (state-side extraction fields, happening-side temporal fields) with no
# materialized closure. A State (fluent) that holds is the middle term between a happening and its
# normative effect: Action/Event initiates State, then State activatesObligation /
# activatesConstraint. OWL-DL safe: subject is Action or Event and object is State, all
# distinct individuals, so no one individual is forced into two disjoint categories.
proeth-core:initiates a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( proeth-core:Action proeth-core:Event ) ] ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:label "initiates"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Event Calculus initiation: relates a happening (Action or Event) to a State (fluent) that begins to hold as a result and persists until terminated (inertial) or holds momentarily (non-inertial)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links an Action or Event to a State (fluent) it initiates."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Kowalski and Sergot (1986). A logic-based calculus of events. New Generation Computing 4(1): 67-95 (doi:10.1007/BF03037383)." ,
                "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. Proceedings of the 16th Conference on Autonomous Agents and MultiAgent Systems (AAMAS 2017): 96-104 (doi:10.65109/hhri9090)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF03037383> , # Kowalski and Sergot 1986
                   <https://doi.org/10.65109/hhri9090> . # Berreby et al. 2017

proeth-core:terminates a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( proeth-core:Action proeth-core:Event ) ] ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:label "terminates"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Event Calculus termination: relates a happening (Action or Event) to a State (fluent) that ceases to hold as a result."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links an Action or Event to a State (fluent) it terminates."@en ;
    iao:0000119 "Kowalski and Sergot (1986). A logic-based calculus of events. New Generation Computing 4(1): 67-95 (doi:10.1007/BF03037383)." ,
                "Berreby et al. (2017). A declarative modular framework for representing and applying ethical principles. Proceedings of the 16th Conference on Autonomous Agents and MultiAgent Systems (AAMAS 2017): 96-104 (doi:10.65109/hhri9090)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF03037383> , # Kowalski and Sergot 1986
                   <https://doi.org/10.65109/hhri9090> . # Berreby et al. 2017

###############################################################
# CodeProvision Properties
# Added: 2026-01-12 - Support for provision-concept linkage
###############################################################

# Relates a provision to the concepts it establishes
proeth-core:establishes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:range [
        a owl:Class ;
        owl:unionOf (proeth-core:Principle proeth-core:Obligation proeth-core:Constraint)
    ] ;
    rdfs:label "establishes"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a code provision to the ethical concepts (principles, obligations, constraints) it establishes or defines."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a CodeProvision to the normative concepts it creates or reinforces."@en ;
    skos:example "NSPE I.1 establishes PublicSafety (Principle), DutyOfCare (Obligation)"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0004-3702(03)00135-8> . # McLaren 2003 - extensional grounding; migrated from the deleted intermediate twin

# Inverse: track which provision establishes a concept
proeth-core:establishedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf proeth-core:establishes ;
    rdfs:domain [
        a owl:Class ;
        owl:unionOf (proeth-core:Principle proeth-core:Obligation proeth-core:Constraint)
    ] ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:label "established by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Indicates which code provision establishes this ethical concept."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a normative concept to the CodeProvision that defines it."@en .

# Relates a provision to its parent guideline
proeth-core:partOfGuideline a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Guideline ;
    rdfs:label "part of guideline"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a code provision to the guideline document it belongs to."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a CodeProvision to its containing Guideline document."@en .

# Inverse: guideline contains provisions
proeth-core:containsProvision a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf proeth-core:partOfGuideline ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Guideline ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:label "contains provision"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a guideline to the provisions it contains."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links a Guideline to the CodeProvisions it contains."@en .

# For case analysis to cite provisions
proeth-core:citesProvision a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain iao:0000030 ;  # IAO:information content entity -- the citing analysis element (conclusion, question, decision record)
    rdfs:range proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-04: rdfs:domain iao:0000030 added (previously rangeless-domain). The citing subjects are analysis records (information content entities); the cases-layer classes that bear the edge (EthicalConclusion et al.) are ICE subclasses, and core cannot reference the cases namespace without inverting the import layering."@en ;
    rdfs:label "cites provision"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an ethical analysis or decision to the code provisions it references."@en ;
    skos:definition "Links an analysis element to the CodeProvision(s) that justify or support it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Used in case analysis to track which provisions are cited when applying ethical principles."@en .

###############################################################
# CodeProvision Datatype Properties
###############################################################

proeth-core:provisionCode a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "provision code"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The alphanumeric code identifying this provision within its guideline (e.g., 'II.1.c', 'P1.3')."@en ;
    skos:definition "The unique identifier code for a provision within its parent guideline."@en .

proeth-core:provisionText a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "provision text"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The full normative text of the provision."@en ;
    skos:definition "The complete text content of the code provision."@en .

proeth-core:provisionCategory a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:CodeProvision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "provision category"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The category or section type within the guideline (e.g., 'fundamental_canons', 'rules_of_practice', 'professional_obligations')."@en ;
    skos:definition "Classification of the provision within the guideline structure."@en .

###############################################################
# IAO Document Integration for Resources
# Added: 2025-10-07 - Support for linking Resources to IAO documents
###############################################################

# Link Resource to IAO document (deprecated 2026-07-07; never emitted)
proeth-core:refersToDocument a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Resource ;
    rdfs:range iao:0000310 ;  # IAO_0000310 = document (was iao:0000300, which is "textual entity")
    rdfs:label "refers to document"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a resource to the IAO document it represents or references. Deprecated: no emitter ever wrote it and no committed case carries it. The shipped provision linkage for code resources is containsProvision (resolved from provisionCodes designations), and the shipped document-identity carrier on resource individuals is the proeth:documentTitle literal."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Resource to an Information Artifact Ontology document that it represents."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-07-07: marked owl:deprecated; unused (containsProvision carries the provision linkage; the range of this property is an IAO document, not a CodeProvision). Retained for identifier stability."@en .

# Indicate which agent uses or has access to a resource (case context).
# Range broadened Role -> Agent on 2026-05-30: under the Agent model an actor is
# a proeth-core:Agent that bears Role facets, so resource usage is an actor-level
# fact (the same level hasRole / hasCapability / performsAction attach at). This
# property was previously declared but unused in every case; it is now
# materialized at commit by proethica's resource_edges applier from the extracted
# resource `used_by` field (Resource -> the case Agent(s) that use it), with a
# prov:Derivation carrying the verbatim source text.
proeth-core:availableTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Resource ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "available to"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Indicates which Agent(s) in the case scenario use or have access to this resource. Distinguishes resources used by case participants from those cited by analysts (citedByAgent)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Resource to an Agent indicating who uses or can access it within the case scenario."@en .

# Indicate which agent cited a resource (analytic authority)
proeth-core:citedByAgent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Resource ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "cited by agent"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Indicates which Agent(s) cited this resource as an authoritative source in their ethical analysis. Distinguishes citations (the Discussion-section authority signal) from resources used by case participants (availableTo)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Resource to an Agent indicating who cited it as authority."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "2026-05-30: range broadened Role -> Agent for parity with availableTo; citing is an actor-level fact, like resource usage."@en .

# State -> the Agent(s) it affects. Materialized from the state extractor's
# `affectedParties` field (the actors a state bears on), resolved to the case
# Agent(s) by embedding shortlist + LLM multi-select, with a prov:Derivation
# carrying the verbatim party text (added 2026-05-31). Agent is outside the nine
# disjoint D-tuple categories, so a State -> Agent edge is OWL-DL-safe, as with
# availableTo. Generic affected parties ("future occupants and public") match no
# Agent and correctly yield no edge.
proeth-core:affects a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:State ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "affects"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a State to the Agent(s) it bears on in the case scenario (the parties whose situation the state changes), distinct from the obligations/constraints the state activates (activatesObligation / activatesConstraint)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a State to an Agent it affects within the case scenario."@en .

# Participant edges (v2.6.0). Each Pass-2 normative component carries an extracted
# who field naming the actor it bears on -- obligatedParty on the obligation,
# constrainedEntity on the constraint, possessedBy on the capability, invokedBy on
# the principle. The fields are temp_rdf carrier fields resolved at commit by the
# participant_edges applier in ProEthica into these edges (embedding shortlist +
# LLM select, each edge with a prov:Derivation carrying the verbatim party text);
# no intermediate-namespace literal twin is committed (the CMT-3 routing skip).
# Range is Agent, which is outside the nine disjoint D-tuple categories, so each
# edge is OWL-DL safe exactly as availableTo / citedByAgent / affects are (the
# object resolves to no disjoint category; the commit-time guard still validates
# the component subject). A party that matches no case Agent yields no edge, with
# one deterministic exception carried by invokedBy alone among these four: a
# Board-pattern party (the NSPE Board of Ethical Review) resolves to the
# case-scoped NSPE Board Agent minted at commit (citedByAgent, in the actor-edge
# family, carries the same fallback). A Board-pattern party on obligatedParty,
# constrainedEntity, or possessedBy yields no edge.

proeth-core:obligatedParty a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "obligated party"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an Obligation to the Agent that bears it (the by-whom slot of the Richardson 1990 specification scheme, adopted by Dennis et al. 2016). The duty-bearer of the obligation in the case scenario."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Obligation to the Agent who is obligated by it."@en .

proeth-core:constrainedEntity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "constrained entity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a Constraint to the Agent whose conduct it limits in the case scenario. Distinct from constrainedBy (Action -> Constraint), which records that an action is subject to a constraint."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Constraint to the Agent it constrains."@en .

proeth-core:possessedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf proeth-core:hasCapability ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Capability ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "possessed by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a Capability to the Agent that possesses it in the case scenario. Inverse of hasCapability (Agent -> Capability), so the reasoner sees both directions from one asserted edge."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Capability to the Agent who possesses it. Inverse of hasCapability."@en .

proeth-core:invokedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Principle ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "invoked by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a Principle to the Agent(s) that invoke it in the case scenario (the parties who appeal to the principle to justify or evaluate conduct). May name several agents."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Principle to an Agent who invokes it."@en .

###############################################################
# Actor relations (professional/social relationships between role-bearers)
###############################################################
# RO and BFO provide no social relations such as client, peer, or employer, because
# in BFO such relationships are realized through the roles agents bear. The relational
# role archetypes (ProviderClientRole, ProfessionalPeerRole, and so on) carry the
# relationship TYPE; these properties carry the specific counterparty. The five typed
# subproperties carry rdfs:domain/range proeth-core:Role (added v2.10.3 after the
# 2026-07-05 regeneration verified every endpoint corpus-wide is a Role facet; the
# former schema:domainIncludes soft hints, needed while legacy cases attached these
# edges to Agent endpoints against the Agent/Role disjointness, are retired).
# relatedTo alone stays domain-less: it is the commit-time fallback for relationship
# types outside the controlled vocabulary, so it must not inject Role typing onto
# unvetted endpoints, and a domain on the parent would reach every subproperty edge
# via the subPropertyOf entailment anyway.

proeth-core:relatedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "The neutral parent of the actor-relation family. Deliberately without domain or range: it exists so unmapped relationships have a declared home rather than an invented predicate, and it is the commit-time fallback for relation types outside the controlled vocabulary, so it must not inject Role typing onto unvetted endpoints. The typed subproperties (hasClient, employedBy, professionalPeerOf, reviewsWorkOf, workReviewedBy) carry the Role/Role domain and range axioms."@en ;
    rdfs:label "related to"@en ;
    skos:definition "A professional or social relationship between two role-bearing agents whose specific type is not mapped to a more specific actor relation."@en .

proeth-core:hasClient a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf proeth-core:relatedTo ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates the provider-side role facet to the client-side role facet; the bearer Agent on each side is reached via hasRole. An asserted edge assigns the provider side the ProviderClientRole archetype, a defined class inferred from this edge at commit, with the extraction category field as fallback only. Emitted at commit from the extraction relationships field."@en ;
    rdfs:label "has client"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a provider role-bearer to the client for whom it performs professional service."@en .

proeth-core:professionalPeerOf a owl:ObjectProperty, owl:SymmetricProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf proeth-core:relatedTo ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:comment "Symmetric collegial relation between role facets. An asserted edge assigns each side the ProfessionalPeerRole archetype, a defined class inferred from this edge at commit."@en ;
    rdfs:label "professional peer of"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates two role-bearers in a collegial professional-peer relationship, for example mutual review or mentoring. Symmetric."@en .

proeth-core:employedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf proeth-core:relatedTo ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:comment "Employment relation from the employee side, between role facets. An asserted edge assigns the employee side the EmployerRelationshipRole archetype, a defined class inferred from this edge at commit."@en ;
    rdfs:label "employed by"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a role-bearer to the employer it works for."@en .

proeth-core:reviewsWorkOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf proeth-core:relatedTo ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Role ;
    owl:inverseOf proeth-core:workReviewedBy ;
    rdfs:comment "Directed review relation from the reviewer side. Inverse of workReviewedBy."@en ;
    rdfs:label "reviews work of"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a reviewing role-bearer to the role-bearer whose work it reviews."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.10.3: the owl:inverseOf workReviewedBy axiom asserted; earlier versions claimed the inverse in comments without it."@en .

proeth-core:workReviewedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf proeth-core:relatedTo ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Role ;
    rdfs:comment "Directed review relation from the reviewed side; inverse of reviewsWorkOf."@en ;
    rdfs:label "work reviewed by"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a role-bearer to another role-bearer who reviews its work."@en .

###############################################################
# D-tuple-level edges promoted to core (v2.8.0)
# Five relation families moved from the intermediate namespace so each family is
# single-namespace, joining fulfillsObligation / realizesCapability / initiates /
# terminates, which were already core. Each subject and object is among the nine
# disjoint D-tuple categories (Obligation, Principle, Action, Capability), so the
# domain/range clauses are checked by the reasoner against the disjointness backbone.
###############################################################

proeth-core:derivedFromPrinciple a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Principle ;
    rdfs:label "derived from principle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an obligation to the principle or principles it operationalizes. The obligation gives concrete directive force to the more abstract value the principle expresses (Dennis et al. 2016)."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Obligation to the Principle it operationalizes."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.8.0: Promoted from proeth:derivedFromPrinciple (intermediate) to core; semantics unchanged."@en .

proeth-core:violatesObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "violates obligation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to an obligation it breaches. The action fails to realize the directive content of the obligation, the negative counterpart of fulfillsObligation."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Action to an Obligation it violates."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.8.0: Promoted from proeth:violatesObligation (intermediate) to core; semantics unchanged."@en .

# The fulfills/violates/raises buckets partition each action's obligation
# engagement (enforced by the obligation-engagement pass per action); the three
# pairwise disjointness axioms make a same-pair double tag reasoner-visible.
proeth-core:fulfillsObligation owl:propertyDisjointWith proeth-core:violatesObligation .
proeth-core:fulfillsObligation owl:propertyDisjointWith proeth-core:raisesObligation .
proeth-core:violatesObligation owl:propertyDisjointWith proeth-core:raisesObligation .

proeth-core:guidedByPrinciple a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Principle ;
    rdfs:label "guided by principle"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to a principle that directs it. The principle supplies the value the action is taken to serve."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Action to a Principle that guides it."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.8.0: Promoted from proeth:guidedByPrinciple (intermediate) to core; semantics unchanged."@en .

proeth-core:raisesObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Action ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "raises obligation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an action to an obligation it puts at stake without itself resolving it. The fulfillment or breach occurs at a downstream action in the same chain. This third predicate distinguishes an upstream choice that engages an obligation from the downstream action that satisfies or breaches it; without it the same obligation appears as both fulfilled and violated across adjacent steps."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Action to an Obligation it puts in force or at stake, resolved at a downstream action."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.8.0: Promoted from proeth:raisesObligation (intermediate) to core; semantics unchanged."@en .

proeth-core:requiresCapability a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain proeth-core:Obligation ;
    rdfs:range proeth-core:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "requires capability"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an obligation to a capability its discharge presupposes. An obligation can be discharged only by an agent that possesses the required capability, the ought-implies-can principle of deontic logic (Kant's dictum, whose computational analogue Stenseke 2024 develops as moral tractability); in this framework an agent that lacks the required capability leaves the obligation subject to override, recorded via defeasibleUnder."@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates an Obligation to a Capability its discharge presupposes."@en ;
    skos:changeNote "v2.8.0: Promoted to core and re-domained from Role to Capability (intermediate) to Obligation to Capability. The intermediate Role-domain proeth:requiresCapability is deprecated separately (since deleted 2026-07-04 with the other v2.8.0-superseded intermediate stubs)."@en .

###############################################################
# Formal Specification Completeness Constraints
###############################################################

# Obligation-existence is a PROFESSIONAL-role commitment, not a universal Role one:
# proeth:ProfessionalRole in the intermediate layer carries
#   subClassOf [ owl:onProperty hasObligation ; owl:someValuesFrom Obligation ]
# (plus governedByCode some EthicalCode). The former core-level restriction on Role
# was removed in v2.10.0 so participant roles (Client, Owner, affected public) do not
# inherit an obligation-existence commitment their own definitions deny.

# Ensure every action is performed by at least one agent
# Uses named inverse property isPerformedBy (OWL DL compliant, no anonymous inverseOf in restriction).
# Completeness scope: the corpus exhibits this for IN-CASE actions; a discussion-sourced
# action performed by a precedent-case party resolves to no case Agent (no Agents are
# minted for precedent parties), which is a correct open-world none-select, not a gap
# to force-fill (2026-07-07 A/E properties review; a forced match would assert a wrong performer).
proeth-core:Action rdfs:subClassOf [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty proeth-core:isPerformedBy ;
    owl:someValuesFrom proeth-core:Agent
] .

###############################################################
# Enhanced Disjointness Axioms for Ontology Engineering Quality
###############################################################

# Pairwise disjointness within the nine D-tuple components is NOT asserted here:
# it is entailed by the nine-way owl:AllDisjointClasses below, and the pairwise
# duplicates (Role-Capability, P/O/Cs-Resource, Action/Event/State-Resource/Role/
# Capability) were removed 2026-07-04 as redundant. Pairwise axioms remain ONLY
# for classes OUTSIDE the nine (Agent, CodeProvision, Guideline), whose
# separations the nine-way set does not cover.
#
# Agent is NOT folded into the nine-way set (which would entail the same pairs):
# the nine-way set is the D-tuple mutual-exclusion claim itself, and Agent is a
# participant bearer outside the framework, so its separations are kept as this
# ordinary one-subject disjointWith statement. Rationale per pair: material
# entity vs occurrents (Action, Event); vs realizable/dependent entities (Role,
# Capability, State); vs information content entities (Principle, Obligation,
# Constraint, Resource, CodeProvision, Guideline). Asserted explicitly because
# the validation harness strips owl:imports, so the BFO separations are not
# loaded (v2.7.1 note); consolidated from six mixed-direction statements into
# one Agent-subject statement in v2.10.2 (semantics unchanged; owl:disjointWith
# is symmetric).
proeth-core:Agent owl:disjointWith proeth-core:Role, proeth-core:Principle,
    proeth-core:Obligation, proeth-core:State, proeth-core:Resource,
    proeth-core:Action, proeth-core:Event, proeth-core:Capability,
    proeth-core:Constraint, proeth-core:CodeProvision, proeth-core:Guideline .

# The directive STATEMENT class vs the descriptive knowledge-source class (see the
# CodeProvision iao:0000116 boundary note). Guideline is deliberately NOT disjoint
# with Resource: a guideline document is a kind of Resource (v2.7.3), which is also
# why no AllDisjointClasses set can absorb these two -- a set containing both
# Guideline and Resource would make Guideline unsatisfiable.
proeth-core:CodeProvision owl:disjointWith proeth-core:Resource .

# Core formal specification components remain disjoint
[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( proeth-core:Role proeth-core:Principle proeth-core:Obligation 
                proeth-core:State proeth-core:Resource proeth-core:Action 
                proeth-core:Event proeth-core:Capability proeth-core:Constraint ) .

# BFO foundational disjointness preservation
[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( bfo:0000002 bfo:0000003 ) .  # continuant vs occurrent